  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n    Central Idea: We transition from local refinement to global exploration via a multi-resolution strategy and periodic function hypothesis. The key observation is that the optimal h(x) is likely defined by a periodic embedding of lower-periodic functions. We sample function patches at multiple scales (n=560 --> n=280, 140, etc.) using Weierstrass-type interpolation and periodic boundary conditions to traverse the function space efficiently.\n    Motivation: This approach balances diversity across scales with the hope that periodicity minimizes correlation at integer shifts.\n    Trade-offs: Reduced resolution might miss fine-grained structures but allows better statistical sampling. The L2-regularization quantifies tradeoff between fidelity to samples and smoothness.\n\n 2. Mathematical Specification:\n    Objective: Minimize C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx over [0,2]\u00d7[0,2]\n    Representation: \n        Context: Sampled function h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] at n evenly spaced points.\n        Variables: 1D vector h of length n, 0\u2264h[i]\u22641, and \u2211h*dx = 1.\n        Feasibility: Repair function to maintain constraints during gradient-based refinement.\n    Periodicity Hypothesis: Define base function f_p: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] with integral 1 (continuous normalization) and periodically tile it as h(x) = f_p(x - floor(x/a)\u00b7a) where a is the periodic scaling factor.\n    Optimization Procedure:\n        a. Sample candidate base functions from diverse periodic templates (seen sample patterns) interpolated via Chebyshev polynomial of degree m.\n        b. Project sampled function onto target resolution using bilinear scaling\n        c. Refine via projected gradients with explicit L2 penalty on higher derivatives to enforce piecewise smoothness.\n\n 3. Implementation Procedure:\n    a. Initialization Module:\n        i. Given seed, generate random samples of base function parameters (Fourier phases, polynomial coefficients)\n        ii. Transform base function period using geometric beta distribution mean 0.1, std dev 0.05\n        iii. Project onto [0,2] with periodicity enforcement (modulo grid)\n    b. Projection Module:\n        i. Input: Base function parameters, current n, target n (multiple resolution)\n        ii. Output: Sampled function at target resolution\n        Operation:\n            - Compute scaling factor s = target_n / base_n\n            - Interpolate using cubic convolution\n            - Clip and repair to [0,1] and normalization\n        Shape: (target_n,)\n    c. Refinement Module:\n        i. Input: Candidate h, current n\n        ii. Output: Refined candidate, new C5 value\n        Operation:\n            - Compute all global correlation values (mode=\"full\")\n            - Apply projected gradient descent to minimize the Pareto-optimal tradeoff between maximum correlation and L2 smoothness\n            - Gradient computation uses closed-form expressions for both penalized terms\n            - Step size 0.01 to 0.1, adaptive to local curvature\n\n 4. Search Choices:\n    Default Configuration:\n        Base Template Distribution: Mixture of sawtooth, square, ramp and random noise\n        Resolution Reduction: n=560 -> 140,280,420 reduction factor 4,3,2 (default reduction factor 3)\n        Iteration Schedules:\n            Multiresolution cycle: 10 iterations * 3\n            Mean step size: 0.05\n        Schedule Rediscover Phase (hourglass pattern):\n            Every 30 iterations (first 10 at each scale), inject new base function by restarting process.\n        Used Variations (each applying to different reduction factors):\n            C1: Reduction factor 2 (n=280)\n            C2: Reduction factor 3 (n=220)\u0271\n            C3: Reduction factor 4 (n=140)\n        Each variation \u2642 means potential to escape local minima defined by different aperiodicity scale.\n\n 5. Budget and Robustness:\n    Dominant cost: Progressive multi-reduction with 4 scales and 10 explicate gradient iterations.\n    Estimable kernel: Projected gradient descent across scales, termination if time exceeds (80% budget left)\n    Response to solver failure: Automatically drop to reduced scale and continue\n    Periodicity fallback: If all attempts fail, project original periodic base through multiple linear auto-corrections\n\n 6. Validation:\n    Repair constraints: Verify 0<=clip(h)<=1 and repair normalization\n    Lag resolution: Ensure one-sided window overlap in five-point stencil\n    Public check: Final evaluation using same scipy correlate full mode, ensuring exact matching of metric.\n\n   For budget_s enforcement:\n    - Time.sleep(jitter) inside expensive operations to simulate checking\n    - Early termination check in refinement loops\n\n   For numerical instability:\n    - Clip gradient magnitudes to 10 to prevent explosion\n    - Fallback to L-BFGS-B for line-search stability\n\n   For lack of improvement:\n    - Implement progressive gradient inlining via pseudo-random targeting\n    - Use restart based on periodic memory (store best found every hourglass cycle)\n\n   Final fallback candidate construction for no parent:\n    - Generate uniform noise, clip, scale to integral=1, and smooth via Fourier low-pass",
